# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

# A packet ingress path, written with the flowchart ROLE vocabulary
# (`FLOWCHART-ROLE-KEYWORDS`). The point of the figure is that every step says WHAT IT
# IS in a word — `terminator`, `process`, `decision` — so a reader who has
# never opened the FigDown spec can follow it from the syntax alone. The
# drawing is derived from those words and carries none of the meaning.
figdown 0.2 flowchart
title "Packet ingress path"

class dropped "Dropped — the packet leaves the pipeline here" fill=#fee2e2 stroke=#dc2626
class passed  "Passed on — handed to the forwarding stage"    fill=#dcfce7 stroke=#16a34a

# The wire and the next stage are outside this figure (`EXTERNAL-EDGE-ENDPOINTS`: never drawn).
external port  "from the ingress port"
external stage "to the forwarding stage"

terminator rx "Frame received"
process    l2 "Read the L2 header"

# The n-way dispatch. `shape=ellipse` is here on purpose: it is what the
# production corpus actually does to a wide dispatch, because an ellipse is
# narrower than a diamond and the branch labels fit around it. Under the old
# spelling (`node … shape=ellipse`) that layout choice DELETED the fact that
# this is a branch point — the reader saw an oval and had no way back to
# "decision". With the role as a word the drawing is free and the meaning is
# safe: the model says decision, the ellipse says only "narrow".
decision ethertype "EtherType?" shape=ellipse

# The five outcomes of the dispatch, in the order they are drawn left to
# right: the three protocols this stage understands, the fallback, and last
# the one branch that comes back (see the back-edge below).
process    ipv4   "Check IPv4 header"
process    ipv6   "Check IPv6 header"
process    arp    "Answer or relay ARP"
terminator ignore "Drop:\nunknown type" class=dropped
process    vlan   "Pop one VLAN tag"

# A binary decision, drawn the conventional way because nothing here needs a
# different picture. Same keyword, different shape — and the keyword is what a
# reading agent uses, in both figures.
decision csum "IPv4 checksum OK?"

terminator drop    "Drop:\nbad checksum" class=dropped
terminator forward "Forward"             class=passed

flow down
# The five dispatch outcomes are PEERS — one of them fires, never two.
rank ipv4,ipv6,arp,ignore,vlan

flowline port -> rx
flowline rx -> l2
flowline l2 -> ethertype

# Five exits, each labelled with the VALUE that selects it, plus a fallback.
# The labels are the only thing that says which branch fires — not the shape,
# not the colour, not where the box landed on the canvas.
flowline ethertype -[0x0800 IPv4]-> ipv4
flowline ethertype -[0x86dd IPv6]-> ipv6
flowline ethertype -[0x0806 ARP]-> arp
flowline ethertype -[other]-> ignore
flowline ethertype -[0x8100 VLAN]-> vlan

# The loop: after popping a tag the EtherType is read again, which is how a
# double-tagged frame unwinds. A plain back-edge IS the loop — FigDown has no
# loop keyword, and one would state nothing this line does not.
flowline vlan -[re-read]-> ethertype

flowline ipv4 -> csum
flowline csum -[no]-> drop
flowline csum -[yes]-> forward
flowline ipv6 -> forward
flowline arp -> forward
flowline forward -> stage
